{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q020_syntaxin_fragment_reclassification","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08 the current treatment does not move 1br0 or 1ez3 to a different fold. All three records (d1br0a_, d1ez3a_, d1ez3b_) and the 1dn1 chain B record (d1dn1b_) sit in a.47.2.1: fold a.47 (STAT-like), superfamily a.47.2 (t-snare proteins), family a.47.2.1. The change is in how the fragments are annotated, not in fold identity. The 1br0 A and 1ez3 A/B records carry the comment 'three-helical fragment; similar to one spectrin repeat' and a heterogeneity flag 'ms' (missing some secondary structures that made up less than one-third of the common domain). So fragment-based fold assignments from these structures should be read as evidence for the N-terminal three-helical region only. They support family-level placement of that region in a.47.2.1, but they say nothing about the full domain's architecture, its boundaries, or whether other secondary-structure elements are present.

1dn1 chain B (d1dn1b_) has a 222-residue genetic SEQRES record, against 120 for d1br0a_ and 124 for d1ez3a_. Its first ~118 residues match the 1br0 A record before diverging, and it continues for about 100 more residues. It has no fragment comment and no 'ms' flag, so it is the longer-coverage record. Its provenance is weaker, though. Its protein-level node is 'automated matches' (sunid 191190), with the comment 'automated match to d3c98b_'. The 1br0, 1ez3 and d3c98b_ records use the curated node 'Syntaxin 1A N-terminal domain' (sunid 47663). The PDB entry 1dn1 is obsolete in 2.08 (obsoleted by 3c98), and d3c98b_ is the curated representative with no caveats. The correct reading is therefore: use 1dn1 chain B to see that the fragment records cover part of a longer N-terminal segment, but do not treat it as an authoritative full-domain boundary. Do not use it to upgrade the fragments into full-domain assignments, and do not use the fragments to dispute the 1dn1 placement. 1ez3 A is a few residues longer than 1br0 A yet still carries the fragment label, so SCOPe's fragment caveat is annotation-driven, not a length rule.

Limits: I queried only release 2.08, so I cannot verify any historical reclassification of these entries, and I did not confirm the 'early fragment' status against older releases. The reason a classification changed, if one did, is not in these data and would need the earlier release records and the associated literature. I did not inspect any structure or residue-level alignment, so the sequence overlap is not a structural mapping. The spectrin-repeat comment is a similarity note, not a homology assertion. Shared a.47.2.1 placement supports common ancestry at the superfamily/family level only, and it does not establish function or a specific structural template. No quality records were retrieved, so no representative was ranked by SPACI, AEROSPACI or resolution.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info: /api/v1/releases/current (release 2.08)","mcp__scope__get_scop_pdb_entry: /api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1br0/domains, /api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1ez3/domains, /api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1dn1 (obsolete_date 2008-03-25, obsoleted_by 3c98), /api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1dn1/domains","mcp__scope__get_scop_domains: /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1br0a_/parents, /annotations, /homology; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ez3a_/parents, /annotations, /homology; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1ez3b_/parents, /annotations, /homology; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1dn1b_/parents, /annotations, /homology; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d3c98b_/parents, /annotations, /homology","mcp__scope__get_scop_sequence: /api/v1/sequences/sids/d1br0a_?release=2.08&source=seqres&style=genetic&format=json; /api/v1/sequences/sids/d1dn1b_?release=2.08&source=seqres&style=genetic&format=json; /api/v1/sequences/sids/d1ez3a_?release=2.08&source=seqres&style=genetic&format=json","scop-homology/SKILL.md"]}]}
